    "summary": "Scarlet>Fire climax, solid acoustic set, mixed tape quality",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a strong acoustic first set with Dire Wolf and Ripple, followed by a powerful electric stretch highlighted by the Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain sequence. China Cat Sunflower into I Know You Rider and an upbeat U.S. Blues encore add extra sparkle. While audience recordings vary in fidelity, the performance is consistently praised for Jerry’s golden tone and tight band interplay.",
    "review": "On October 22, 1980 at the Warfield, the Dead rolled out a three‑set marathon that balances folk‑rock intimacy with electric firepower. Set 1 opens on an acoustic high note – Dire Wolf and Ripple showcase Garcia’s shimmering guitar tone and the group’s tight vocal harmonies. The second set builds momentum with Alabama Getaway into a classic Greatest Story Ever Told, though the pacing dips around Friend Of The Devil and Peggy‑O. The real highlight arrives in Set 3: a searing Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain transition that lifts the band to a euphoric peak before diving into an exploratory jam through Lost Sailor, Saint Of Circumstance, and Space. A rousing encore of U.S. Blues caps the night.\n\nRecording-wise the available tapes are all audience sources with varying fidelity. The “gd80-10‑22.sony.wiley” tape (the recommended version) is only fair in quality – low‑generation tape, uneven levels and prominent crowd chatter – but it captures the concert vibe and includes the full setlist. A second audience recording offers clearer room presence yet runs slightly fast and even drops a four‑minute drum segment, while a third source provides no concrete details. Despite these flaws, fans repeatedly note Garcia’s “golden” tone, Phil’s solid bass foundation, and an overall locked‑in band feel, making this show worth seeking for its musical moments rather than pristine sound.",
